{"abstract":"This chapter applies science identity to the notion of talent in higher education science: What it is and who it is for. The study draws on interview data produced to explore what teachers and students value and recognize as ideal and celebrated student characteristics and practices. One dataset is informed by students enrolled at a highly selective master’s science programme where women are majority. The other brings a teacher-perspective using interviews with undergraduate science faculty at the same institution. A thematic analysis that operationalises talent as an ideal, exposes a gendered mechanism for in- and ex- clusion that determine what and who are recognized and privileged in science. The results are treated as two distinct themes: (1) what talent is and is not, locates talent as hegemonically masculine and; (2) the price of talent, investigates how personal sacrifice associates science identity with talent. Together the two themes suggest that feminine performativity, within or outside of science, will not be read as talent. Instead, interviews show that when ‘talent’ is used to describe ideals that associate with science identities, it describes masculinized ideals that work to position students and teachers relative to each other in ways that renders science inherently inequitable.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Holmegaard, Henriette Tolstrup","Johannsen, Bjørn Friis"],"cuid":["S02-081.67-666727"],"filters":{"added":"2023-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Holmegaard, Henriette Tolstrup"],"keyword":["Equity","Gender","Science identity","Talent"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci","soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-KP"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-UC-KP"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"science talent and unlimited devotion an investigation of the dynamics of university students science identities through the lens of gendered conceptualisations of talent","src":["kp","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-UC-KP"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85152357446","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-081.67-666727","identifier":[{"id":"85152357446","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["kp-08d9ff9c-c613-4b4a-8305-1315c693f331","ku-f447c454-33ca-4e50-b233-85c3417088f5"],"keyword":["Celebrated student","Equity","Gender","Higher edcation","Ideal student","Science identity","Talent"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology","Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-E31EF79F33EB0BAA084882926A430C75"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Science Education","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Science Education, Equity in Science Education","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Science Education, University Science Education"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"kp university college copenhagen","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-KP","l3nl":"KP","l3nn":"KP University College Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KP University College Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Professionshøjskole, Rektors område, Fakultet for læreruddannelse og livslang læring - Dekan, Læreruddannelsen Forskning og Udvikling","Læring, dannelse og trivsel i skolen – Institut for Læreruddannelse ","Læring, dannelse og trivsel i skolen – Institut for Læreruddannelse , Udvikling af fagdidaktik i skolen, STEM-didaktik"],"nora_code":"DI-UC-KP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Henriette Tolstrup","key":"holmegaardpau","last":"Holmegaard","locid":"bc622573-dbe6-4d64-ace0-6fe07399c353","name":"Holmegaard, Henriette Tolstrup","orcid":"0000-0003-4245-3983","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Bjørn Friis","key":"johannsenpau","last":"Johannsen","locid":"dc62d99e-7e44-46c5-b3f3-4c43882cada6","name":"Johannsen, Bjørn Friis","orcid":"0000-0001-8412-290X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Henriette Tolstrup","key":"holmegaardpedh","last":"Holmegaard","name":"Holmegaard, Henriette Tolstrup","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Henriette","key":"tolstrupholmegaardpedh","last":"Tolstrup Holmegaard","name":"Tolstrup Holmegaard, Henriette","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Louise","key":"archerpedh","last":"Archer","name":"Archer, Louise","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-17642-5_6","eisbn":["9783031176425"],"isbn":["9783031176418","9783031176449"],"issn":["22133623"],"pages":"113-140","place":"Cham","publisher":"Springer","series":"Contributions from Science Education Research","status":"p","subtitle":"Theory, method and research","title":"Science Identities"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85152357446"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","KP","Universities","University Colleges","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["kp-08d9ff9c-c613-4b4a-8305-1315c693f331","ku-f447c454-33ca-4e50-b233-85c3417088f5"],"src":["KP University College Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Science Talent and Unlimited Devotion : An Investigation of the Dynamics of University Students’ Science Identities Through the Lens of Gendered Conceptualisations of Talent","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-081.67-666727","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Macrophages are important innate immune cells with the ability to adapt their phenotype to environmental cues. Research on human macrophages often uses monocyte-derived macrophages cultured in vitro, but it is unclear if culture medium affects macrophage phenotype. The objective of this study was to determine the impact of culture medium composition on monocyte-derived macrophage phenotype. Monocyte-derived macrophages were generated in different formulations of culture media (RPMI 1640, DMEM, MEM, McCoy's 5a and IMDM). Viability, yield and cell size were monitored, and RT-qPCR, flow cytometry or ELISA was used to compare levels of phenotype markers (CD163, CD206, CD80, TNFα, IL-10, SIRPα, LILRB1 and Siglec-10). Yield, cell size, gene expression, membrane protein levels and release of soluble proteins were all affected by changes in culture medium composition. The most pronounced effects were observed after culture in DMEM, which lacks the non-essential amino acids asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid and proline. Supplementation of DMEM with non-essential amino acids either fully or partly reversed most effects of DMEM on macrophage phenotype. The results suggest culture medium composition and amino acid availability affect the phenotype of human monocyte-derived macrophages cultured in vitro.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Antonsen, Kristian W","Friis, Henriette Nymark","Sorensen, Boe Sandahl","Etzerodt, Anders","Moestrup, Søren Kragh","Møller, Holger Jon"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-674535"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Møller, Holger Jon"],"keyword":["Amino Acids metabolism","Flow Cytometry","Flow Cytometry methods","Human","Humans","Macrophage","Macrophages","Monocytes","Phenotype"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"comparison of culture media reveals that non essential amino acids strongly affect the phenotype of human monocyte derived macrophages","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/imm.13670","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-674535","identifier":[{"id":"85161642805","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37291897","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-111abdfd-0ef7-4483-895e-b2ca771e19b5"],"keyword":["Amino Acids/metabolism","Culture Media/metabolism","Flow Cytometry/methods","Humans","Macrophages","Monocytes","Phenotype","flow cytometry","human","macrophage"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-46388AB00F3752BEECCE818E3AD938A7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/imm.13670","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/406497750/Immunology_-_2023_-_Antonsen_-_Comparison_of_culture_media_reveals_that_non_essential_amino_acids_strongly_affect_the.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of clinical biochemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kristian W","key":"antonsenpau","last":"Antonsen","locid":"01e17620-66af-45bb-be1a-cdddd6e76a1e","name":"Antonsen, Kristian W","orcid":"0000-0002-1867-2222","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henriette N","key":"friispau","last":"Friis","locid":"e4528bf0-355f-4958-9d23-99d01d6d27d9","name":"Friis, Henriette Nymark","name_org":"Friis, Henriette N","orcid":"0009-0001-5089-1687","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Boe S","key":"sorensenpau","last":"Sorensen","locid":"df36b88d-a4e0-49db-9e8e-78f8dc2120c0","name":"Sorensen, Boe Sandahl","name_org":"Sorensen, Boe S","orcid":"0000-0002-9472-8099","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"etzerodtpau","last":"Etzerodt","locid":"c0b60588-6275-45c0-8ad2-bb91a248ff22","name":"Etzerodt, Anders","orcid":"0000-0002-6757-2068","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren K","key":"moestruppau","last":"Moestrup","locid":"84b91217-5ed8-4507-8a92-00c1815e2c81","name":"Moestrup, Søren Kragh","name_org":"Moestrup, Søren K","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Holger J","key":"møllerpau","last":"Møller","locid":"b08dc9ce-d595-49c6-833a-4ec9b4223a05","name":"Møller, Holger Jon","name_org":"Møller, Holger J","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37291897"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/imm.13670","issn":["00192805","09534954"],"issn_org":["00192805"],"issue":"3","journal":"Immunology","pages":"344-358","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell Publishing Ltd","status":"p","title":"Immunology","volume":"170"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85161642805"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-111abdfd-0ef7-4483-895e-b2ca771e19b5"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Comparison of culture media reveals that non-essential amino acids strongly affect the phenotype of human monocyte-derived macrophages","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-674535","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"En første afdækning af børn og unges science-kapital peger på en række muligheder i skolernes undervisning til at understøtte og udvikle, at børnene kan forholde sig mere bredt til den teknologiske og videnskabelige udvikling og de samfundsmæssige og etiske problemstillinger, den rejser. Den påpeger også muligheder for, at undervisningen tydeligere bliver knyttet til børnenes liv og verdenen omkring dem. Baggrund: Både i Danmark og internationalt er der et stærkt fokus på at fremme børn og unges interesse for science. Formålet er, at børn og unge generelt bliver bedre rustet til at tage kritisk stilling til den videnskabelige og teknologiske udvikling. Samtidig er der et ønske fra flere sider om, at flere skal vælge en uddannelse og karrierevej inden for science. SCOPE er et nationalt forskningsprojekt igangsat og finansieret af VILLUM FONDEN og Novo Nordisk Fonden, og SCOPE bidrager nu til disse formål med det første systematiske indblik i danske børn og unges science-kapital. Rapporten belyser også den naturfaglige undervisningspraksis i grundskoler og på ungdomsuddannelser – herunder i hvor høj grad denne praksis præges af, hvad vi kalder science-kapitalfremmende undervisning. Resultater Hvad der sker i familien, er vigtigt for science-kapitalen Undersøgelsen peger på, at der er en stærk sammenhæng mellem det hjemlige miljø og børn og unges science-kapital. Har man samtaler i familien om verden i bred forstand og om undervisningen i skolen, og laver man udadrettede aktiviteter som fx museumsbesøg, er der større sandsynlighed for, at barnet har en høj science-kapital - også selvom disse aktiviteter ikke handler om science. Undersøgelsen peger også på, at de science-relaterede aktiviteter, der foregår i familierne, i høj grad har et dannelsesfokus – og ikke et uddannelsesfokus. Fokus er på fælles oplevelser og erfaringer, der rækker ud over science i sig selv, og der er fx ikke fokus på, at børnene skal kende fagord og begreber. … men det er svært at genkende og værdisætte science-kapitalen i skolen Undersøgelsen viser, at naturfagsundervisningen præges af et stærkt lærerstyret fokus på det faglige indhold, hvor elevernes selvstændige arbejde er mere efterprøvende end undersøgende. Undervisning, som trækker på elevernes kreativitet og undersøgelseskompetencer, lader ikke til at fylde så meget. Eleverne karakteriserer da også selv den gode elev i naturfagene som en, der er stille, hører efter og ved noget på forhånd. Og det handler i høj grad om begrebsforståelse og begrebskendskab. Har du det rette sprog med hjemmefra, er dit udgangspunkt for deltagelse bedre. Eftersom det i høj grad er mere dannelsesorienterede erfaringer, eleverne har med hjemmefra, kan det måske være vanskeligt at veksle disse erfaringer til noget, der i skolesammenhæng tilskrives værdi som science-kapital. Det er også svært at veksle science-kapital mellem skole og det øvrige liv Børnene oplever også, at de har svært ved at se, at det, de lærer i skolen, har noget med dem selv at gøre uden for klasselokalet. Den science-kapital, de bygger op i skolesammenhæng, er således heller ikke let at veksle til noget værdifuldt uden for skolekonteksten. At børn og unge kan se, at naturvidenskab har noget med dem at gøre, og at de kan se sig selv og oplever at blive set i naturvidenskabelige sammenhænge, handler om muligheden for at opbygge en science-identitet. Hvis de ikke oplever naturvidenskab som noget for dem, kan det ud over at have betydning for læringspotentialet også få konsekvenser for deres senere valg af uddannelse. Undersøgelsen viser, at der stort set ikke er forskel på drenge og pigers science-kapitalscore, men at flere drenge end piger har en stærk science-identitet. Kan der bygges en bro mellem skole og hverdagsliv? Undersøgelsen peger samlet set på, at der kan være potentiale i i højere grad at bygge bro mellem skole og hverdagsliv. I naturfagsundervisningen kan man måske blive bedre til at bygge bro fra hverdagslivet til skolen ved at genkende og værdisætte den science-kapital, som eleverne bærer med sig. Man kan måske også i naturfagsundervisningen blive bedre til at bygge bro fra skolen til hverdagslivet, så eleverne oplever, at det bliver lettere at veksle den kapital, de bygger op i skolen, til noget værdifuldt, som de kan bruge i deres hverdag. Det er relevant for alle eleverne, men nok ikke mindst for pigerne, som ser ud til at have særlig svært ved at finde interesse for naturfagene i sig selv. Metode: SCOPE er baseret på et meget omfattende kvantitativt og kvalitativt datagrundlag. Der indgår således spørgeskemabesvarelser fra knap 20.000 elever, knap 1000 lærere og godt 5000 forældre. Der er desuden gennemført interview i SS familier og med SS elever samt gennemført workshops med elever og etnografisk feltarbejde på SS skoler.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Pedersen, Hanne Søndergård","Hindsholm, Martin","Mikkelsen, Maria Falk","Holmegaard, Henriette T.","Nielsen, Katia B.","Ulriksen, Lars","Vixø, Kathrine","Blomgreen, Camilla B.","Friis-Hansen, Mette","Nielsen, Sanne S.","Christiansen, Nadia M.","Jakobsen, Lars S."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-697631"],"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Naturfag","Science kapital","Undervisning"],"level":"com","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-VIVE"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-VIVE","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"børn og unges science kapital baselinerapport","src":["vive"],"src-code":["DI-GV-VIVE"],"type":"dr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.vive.dk/ws/files/24021986/Astra_Scope_Onepager_2023.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-697631","ids":["vive-b66bbcc2-e8ff-4678-b326-54b6df6c5129"],"keyword":["Naturfag","Science-kapital","undervisning"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Commissioned","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-4B037B781DAB0D211D4C616A00C98A59"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.vive.dk/ws/files/24021986/Astra_Scope_Onepager_2023.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.vive.dk/ws/files/26455897/Scope_SCICAP_Onepager_2024_Digital.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.vive.dk/ws/files/24295692/301394_B_rn_og_unges_science-kapital_pdf-ua.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"vive the danish center for social science research","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-VIVE","l3nn":"VIVE The Danish Center for Social Science Research","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"VIVE The Danish Center for Social Science Research","namevar":["VIVE - The Danish Center for Social Science Research, VIVE Ledelsessekretariat"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-VIVE","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Hanne Søndergård","key":"pedersenhannesøndergårdpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"5c0c5d4a-5bd1-4ef8-8865-f487ca629f3e","name":"Pedersen, Hanne Søndergård","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Martin","key":"hindsholmmartinpau","last":"Hindsholm","locid":"d171c926-f5df-4d1d-a2e7-838c29fa634b","name":"Hindsholm, Martin","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Maria Falk","key":"mikkelsenmariafalkpau","last":"Mikkelsen","locid":"ea9db4f9-37b6-4634-92ff-270d325f8a3a","name":"Mikkelsen, Maria Falk","orcid":"0000-0001-6256-6403","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Henriette T.","key":"holmegaardhenriettetpau","last":"Holmegaard","name":"Holmegaard, Henriette T.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Katia B.","key":"nielsenkatiabpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Katia B.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars","key":"ulriksenlarspau","last":"Ulriksen","name":"Ulriksen, Lars","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kathrine","key":"vixøkathrinepau","last":"Vixø","locid":"cea2e636-bc0c-4e3d-bab6-81df1fe040f4","name":"Vixø, Kathrine","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Camilla B.","key":"blomgreencamillabpau","last":"Blomgreen","name":"Blomgreen, Camilla B.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mette","key":"friishansenmettepau","last":"Friis-Hansen","locid":"3907f491-7b05-49ff-b4c6-c2f8b93fa546","name":"Friis-Hansen, Mette","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Sanne S.","key":"nielsensannespau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Sanne S.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Nadia M.","key":"christiansennadiampau","last":"Christiansen","name":"Christiansen, Nadia M.","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars S.","key":"jakobsenlarsspau","last":"Jakobsen","name":"Jakobsen, Lars S.","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"174","publisher":"VIVE - Det Nationale Forsknings- og Analysecenter for Velfærd","repno":"VIVE Rapport","series":"VIVE Rapport","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["vive-b66bbcc2-e8ff-4678-b326-54b6df6c5129"],"src":["VIVE The Danish Center for Social Science Research"],"title":"Børn og unges science-kapital : Baselinerapport","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-697631","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: To study whether endometriosis is associated with pregnancy loss and recurrent pregnancy loss (RPL). DESIGN: Nationwide historical cohort study with a nested case-control analysis. SETTING: National health registers. PATIENT(S): A total of 29,563 women born between 1957 and 1997 were identified in the national health registers, diagnosed with endometriosis between 1977 and 2017, and age-matched 1:10 with 295,630 women without endometriosis. The number of pregnancy losses was assessed, and data were analyzed with conditional logistic regression. INTERVENTION(S): Endometriosis (International Classification of Diseases, 8th Revision, 62530-62539, and International Classification of Diseases, 10th Revision, DN80.0-9). MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S): The primary outcomes of interest were the numbers of pregnancy losses categorized as 0, 1, 2, and ≥ 3 losses, unadjusted and adjusted for gravidity, and RPL. The secondary outcome measures were the predefined types of pregnancy losses. Pregnancy loss was defined as the spontaneous demise of a pregnancy until 22 weeks of gestation. Primary RPL was defined as 3 or more consecutive pregnancy losses with no prior live birth or stillbirth, and secondary RPL was defined as 1 or more births followed by 3 or more consecutive losses. RESULT(S): A total of 18.9%, 3.9%, and 2.1% of ever-pregnant women with endometriosis had 1, 2, and ≥ 3 pregnancy losses compared with 17.3%, 3.5%, and 1.5% of the women without endometriosis, corresponding to the odds ratios of 1.13 (95% confidence interval, 1.09-1.17), 1.18 (1.10-1.26), and 1.44 (1.31-1.59), respectively. When adjusted also for gravidity, the corresponding results were 1.37 (95% confidence interval, 1.32-1.42), 1.75 (1.62-1.89), and 2.57 (2.31-2.85), respectively. The following predefined subgroups of RPL were positively associated with endometriosis: primary; secondary; secondary after giving birth to a boy; after a complicated delivery; and ≥ 3 pregnancy losses before the age of 30 years. Six endometriosis subgroup analyses found an association between endometriosis and pregnancy loss. These analyses were women diagnosed in the 4 decades between 1977 and 2017, women with adenomyosis, and women with adenomyosis only. CONCLUSION(S): This nationwide cohort study found endometriosis to be associated with pregnancy loss and RPL, and the association strengthened with an increasing number of losses.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Boje, Amalie Dyhrberg","Egerup, Pia","Westergaard, David","Mathilde Friis Bertelsen, Marie-Louise","Nyegaard, Mette","Hartwell, Dorthe","Lidegaard, Øjvind","Nielsen, Henriette Svarre"],"cuid":["S02-097.55-672403"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Endometriosis","Pregnancy loss","Recurrent pregnancy loss","Reproduction"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"endometriosis is associated with pregnancy loss a nationwide historical cohort study","src":["aau","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?scp=85150856105&partnerID=8YFLogxK","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-097.55-672403","identifier":[{"id":"85150856105","type":"scopus"},{"id":"36608920","type":"pmid"},{"id":"000999044400001","type":"wos"}],"ids":["aau-9b9e2869-a5ca-486c-81ee-db8aaf3c4f2d","ku-05fdb469-d799-4b9b-9398-7909e64bdcce"],"keyword":["Endometriosis","pregnancy loss","recurrent pregnancy loss","reproduction"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-C30A7E281A9C39DE96F55190C2C8C752"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"cap